{
  "id": "merge-k-sorted-lists",
  "title": "Merge k Sorted Lists",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "categories": ["heap", "linked-list"],
  "descriptionMd": "You receive an array `lists` holding the heads of `k` singly linked lists. Each list is sorted in non-decreasing order.\n\nMerge all of them into **a single sorted linked list** and return its head.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nlists = [[1, 4, 5], [1, 3, 4], [2, 6]]\n→ [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6]\n```\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nlists = []\n→ []\n```\n\n**Example 3**\n\n```\nlists = [[]]\n→ []\n```\nThe array holds one list, and that list is empty.\n\n**Follow-up:** with `N` total nodes, can you beat merging the lists one after another into a growing result?",
  "constraints": ["0 ≤ k ≤ 10⁴", "0 ≤ length of each list ≤ 500", "-10⁴ ≤ Node.val ≤ 10⁴", "Each list is sorted in non-decreasing order.", "The total number of nodes is at most 10⁴."],
  "hints": ["The next node in the output is always one of the current heads — but there can be many heads to compare.", "Which data structure hands you the smallest of k candidates quickly, while letting you add a replacement?", "Another angle: merging lists in pairs, round after round, also avoids redundant work."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "mergeKLists",
    "params": [{ "name": "lists", "type": "ListNode[]" }],
    "returns": "ListNode"
  },
  "comparator": "exact",
